On 9 July 2010 16:20, Oleksii Melnyk <lamelnyk@gmail.com> wrote:

>
>
> 2010/7/8 Luke Bergen <lukeabergen@gmail.com>
>
> What's wrong with {bancu lo kamvrude jo'u lo kampalci}?
>>
>
> I just feel, that the book with "jo'u" is about the distinction between 2
> separate entities, while with "jo'e" is about "existence" of (the set of)
> both.
>

The key is the dichotomy. The title has to express the distinction between
"good" and "evil" that Nietzsche meant to go beyond with his argument.



> In the context of "...no universal morality, that distinguishes...", with
> "jo'u"  we still can have the "...universal morality that joins/mixes...",
> while with "jo'e" we are denying both


The things connected with "jo'u" is unmixed; they are "joined" but not
mixed. A mixed pair is made with "joi".


So with "lo" -- are we speaking about 2 separate entities, or the whole
> abstraction of "(.*)ness"?
>

"bancu"s x2 must be the 2 separate abstract entities (good and evil) the
distinction of which old-school philosophers had tried to make, and x1 must
be something with no such distinction.

